WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.504
From the University of Tokyo, NeurIPS 2022: the
Langevin autoencoder.

00:00:04.584 --> 00:00:08.588
Langevin dynamics samples the intractable
posteriors of deep latent models,

00:00:08.668 --> 00:00:12.672
but slowly.

00:00:12.752 --> 00:00:16.756
The authors amortize it into a shared encoder,
prove it valid MCMC,

00:00:16.836 --> 00:00:20.840
and beat variational autoencoders.

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:28.248
Training deep latent variable models by maximum
likelihood needs an expectation over the latent posterior,

00:00:28.328 --> 00:00:29.656
which is intractable.

00:00:29.736 --> 00:00:33.144
Langevin dynamics samples that posterior accurately,

00:00:33.224 --> 00:00:37.100
but runs a fresh chain per data point and converges
slowly.

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:46.018
Variational inference dominates thanks to
amortization: one shared encoder predicts the latents for all data.

00:00:46.098 --> 00:00:50.446
But it relies on tractable Gaussians, limiting accuracy.

00:00:50.526 --> 00:00:55.269
MCMC has no such limit, yet nobody truly amortized
it before.

00:00:55.349 --> 00:00:59.460
The goal: amortized efficiency with MCMC flexibility.

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:02.711
Two contributions.

00:01:02.791 --> 00:01:05.663
First,

00:01:05.743 --> 00:01:08.614
amortized Langevin dynamics: an MCMC algorithm that
replaces per-datapoint iterations

00:01:08.694 --> 00:01:11.566
with Langevin updates on a shared encoder's parameters,

00:01:11.646 --> 00:01:14.517
proven valid.

00:01:14.597 --> 00:01:17.469
Second, the Langevin autoencoder,

00:01:17.549 --> 00:01:20.420
a generative model that is a small tweak of a
standard autoencoder.

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:25.597
The key idea: move randomness from latent space to
encoder parameters.

00:01:25.677 --> 00:01:29.289
A deterministic encoder maps observations to latents;

00:01:29.369 --> 00:01:32.215
updating its parameters moves the samples.

00:01:32.295 --> 00:01:39.112
Convergence is guaranteed when a fixed feature
extractor feeds a linear layer wider than the batch.

00:01:39.192 --> 00:01:43.292
The autoencoder runs a few steps before each
decoder update,

00:01:43.372 --> 00:01:45.800
optionally with Metropolis-Hastings.

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:50.064
Validation comes in two stages.

00:01:50.144 --> 00:01:54.028
First,

00:01:54.108 --> 00:01:57.992
toy problems with known answers: a
bivariate-Gaussian posterior and a random-network posterior.

00:01:58.072 --> 00:02:01.956
Then image generation on MNIST, S-V-H-N, CIFAR-10,
and CelebA,

00:02:02.036 --> 00:02:05.920
scored by negative ELBO per dimension over three seeds.

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:10.362
On toy problems, amortized Langevin dynamics
reproduces multimodal,

00:02:10.442 --> 00:02:14.504
correlated posteriors that variational inference misses.

00:02:14.584 --> 00:02:18.646
On images,

00:02:18.726 --> 00:02:22.788
the Langevin autoencoder achieves the lowest
negative ELBO on all four datasets.

00:02:22.868 --> 00:02:26.930
The lesson: more accurate posterior sampling yields
better models.

00:02:27.310 --> 00:02:30.906
Two ablations.

00:02:30.986 --> 00:02:34.582
Encoder capacity confirms the theory: when the
final linear layer is at least the batch size,

00:02:34.662 --> 00:02:38.258
samples match; smaller, some collapse.

00:02:38.338 --> 00:02:41.934
On images, the Metropolis-Hastings step stabilizes
training,

00:02:42.014 --> 00:02:45.610
while iteration count barely matters beyond two.

00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:48.809
The gains are consistent but modest.

00:02:48.889 --> 00:02:53.561
On MNIST, negative ELBO drops to one point one
seven seven,

00:02:53.641 --> 00:02:57.105
versus one point one eight nine for the VAE.

00:02:57.185 --> 00:03:02.099
CIFAR-10, CelebA, and S-V-H-N show similar small
improvements.

00:03:02.179 --> 00:03:06.770
The cost: about two-and-a-quarter times slower than a VAE.

00:03:07.150 --> 00:03:11.407
The takeaway is elegant: move Langevin noise from
the latents to the encoder's parameters,

00:03:11.488 --> 00:03:15.745
so sampling becomes both efficient and flexible.

00:03:15.825 --> 00:03:20.083
Provably valid,

00:03:20.163 --> 00:03:24.420
it looks almost like a standard autoencoder yet
consistently beats variational autoencoders on test likelihood.
